TI  - Entrepreneurial ecosystems 5.0: an integrated framework of Artificial Intelligence, entrepreneurial finance, and policy innovation for sustainable startup growth
JO  - Future Business Journal
PY  - 2026
AB  - Abstract Artificial intelligence (AI), digital finance, and responsive policy landscapes are becoming crucial elements of entrepreneurial ecosystems to support ongoing sustainable startup growth. These drivers have been studied separately, however, and research on the integrated impact of these drivers within entrepreneurial ecosystems is still limited. The Entrepreneurial Ecosystem 5.0 (EE 5.0) framework is examined in an empirical way by studying the relationship between Artificial Intelligence, Entrepreneurial Finance, Policy Innovation, Entrepreneurial Ecosystem Quality, and Sustainable Startup Growth. Based on the Resource-Based View (RBV), Dynamic Capabilities Theory (DCT), Institutional Theory, and Entrepreneurial Ecosystem Theory, five hypotheses were formulated and tested with a quantitative, cross-sectional research design. Data were collected using a structured questionnaire administered to 350 startup founders, SME owners, investors, incubator and accelerator managers, innovation managers, and policymakers. The partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) method was used to assess the measurement and structural models. The results confirm that all three variables, AI, EF, and PI have significant positive impacts on the quality of the entrepreneurial ecosystem, which is significantly and strongly positively linked to sustainable start-up growth and acts as a mediator between the three ecosystem capabilities and Startup Growth. The results offer empirical evidence for the Entrepreneurial Ecosystem 5.0 framework and demonstrate that Entrepreneurial Ecosystem Quality is a vital mechanism that connects the technological capability, financial accessibility, and adaptive governance with sustainable entrepreneurial outcomes. The study sheds light on entrepreneurial ecosystem literature by providing empirical findings of an integrated framework based on Industry 5.0 for strengthening the entrepreneurial ecosystem to support and empower innovation-driven and sustainable entrepreneurs and the different stakeholders involved.
